WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.720
<v Rayyan>Hello Professor Aqeel, how are you?

00:00:01.720 --> 00:00:06.080
<v Professor Aqeel>Alhamdulillah, how do you do, Rayyan, this evening? Good evening to you.

00:00:06.080 --> 00:00:19.000
<v Rayyan>I'm good, I'm good. This evening is very nice. So, today I'm interviewing you about AI and its applications specifically because you are a professor for civil engineering at our university.

00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:28.000
<v Rayyan>And I just wanted to ask you a few questions about it. So, to start us off, I want to ask you, what are the benefits of artificial intelligence in daily life?

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:36.000
<v Professor Aqeel>I would say that artificial intelligence is gradually becoming an invisible partner in our daily lives.

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:53.000
<v Professor Aqeel>Whether we realize it or not, we are already using AI when we navigate roads, communicate, access information, receive medical advice or even manage our daily schedules.

00:00:53.000 --> 00:01:19.000
<v Professor Aqeel>From an engineering perspective, AI is particularly exciting. Rayyan, imagine a bridge that can continuously monitor its own structural health, identify potential cracks and alert engineers before a serious problem develops.

00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:31.000
<v Professor Aqeel>Imagine buildings that automatically optimize energy consumption or traffic systems that predict congestion before it happens.

00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:40.000
<v Rayyan>Okay, so for our second question, I want to ask you, what are the risks of artificial intelligence as it keeps expanding?

00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:58.000
<v Professor Aqeel>Rayyan, you should be knowing, every powerful technology comes with responsibilities. AI is no exception. One major concern is that people may become overly dependent on AI.

00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:12.000
<v Professor Aqeel>If we allow machines to think, analyze and make every decision for us, we may gradually weaken our own ability to question, reason and solve problems independently.

00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:26.000
<v Rayyan>Okay, so for our third question, I want to ask you, is there possible dangers of artificial intelligence and if so, how can they be negated in correct implementation?

00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:40.000
<v Professor Aqeel>Yes, Rayyan, there are potential dangers, particularly when AI is implemented without proper human oversight, ethical guidelines and accountability.

00:02:43.000 --> 00:02:50.000
<v Rayyan>Okay, that's a nice answer. So for our fourth question, what is your take on artificial intelligence in general?

00:02:50.000 --> 00:03:09.000
<v Professor Aqeel>Personally, I'm very enthusiastic about artificial intelligence. I see it as one of the most transformative technologies of our time, comparable in its potential impact to the industrial and digital revolutions.

00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:18.000
<v Professor Aqeel>As an engineering professor, I have witnessed how technology has transformed engineering education and professional practice.

00:03:19.000 --> 00:03:35.000
<v Professor Aqeel>We moved from drawing boards to CAD, from manual calculations to sophisticated simulation software and now towards intelligent systems capable of analyzing enormous amount of data.

00:03:35.000 --> 00:03:49.000
<v Professor Aqeel>So I don't see AI as replacement for engineers, educators or professionals. I see it as an opportunity to redefine what we can achieve.

00:03:51.000 --> 00:04:02.000
<v Rayyan>Okay, that take was really nice. So moving on to the last and most important question, how has artificial intelligence affected the recent generation and their abilities?

00:04:02.000 --> 00:04:14.000
<v Professor Aqeel>Rayyan, I think AI has created both an extraordinary opportunity and a significant challenge for the young generation.

00:04:14.000 --> 00:04:32.000
<v Professor Aqeel>I have one concern as an educator. If a student asks AI to solve an engineering problem and simply copies the solution, the student may complete the assignment but miss the learning.

00:04:32.000 --> 00:04:49.000
<v Professor Aqeel>I believe the young generation should develop three essential abilities. Number one, critical thinking. Number two, creativity. And number three, human intelligence.

00:04:49.000 --> 00:05:00.000
<v Professor Aqeel>I would rather have a student who knows how to question AI intelligently than one who knows how to copy from it efficiently.

00:05:01.000 --> 00:05:04.000
<v Rayyan>Okay, thank you professor for your time.

00:05:05.000 --> 00:05:06.000
<v Professor Aqeel>You're welcome.

00:05:07.000 --> 00:05:08.000
<v Rayyan>Okay, and with that we conclude our interview.
